  <h2>Recommended Slab Thickness by Project Type</h2>
  <p>
    Selecting the appropriate slab thickness ensures structural longevity and prevents premature cracking:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sidewalks, Walkways & Garden Paths (4 inches):</strong> A 4-inch pour over a compacted gravel sub-base provides plenty of support for standard pedestrian foot traffic and lawnmowers.</li>
    <li><strong>Patios & Outdoor Living Areas (4 inches):</strong> 4 inches is standard for dining tables, fire pits, and grills. If supporting an outdoor stone fireplace or heavy brick pillars, thicken the edges or pour dedicated footings.</li>
    <li><strong>Shed Foundations & Hot Tub Pads (4 to 6 inches):</strong> Small tool sheds (under 100 sq ft) perform well at 4 inches. Hot tub pads, swim spas, and heavy utility workshops should be poured at 5 to 6 inches with #4 rebar reinforcement.</li>
    <li><strong>Residential Driveways & Garage Floors (4 to 5 inches):</strong> Cars, SUVs, and light pickup trucks require a minimum 4-inch slab with 4,000 PSI concrete and wire mesh or rebar. For regular parking of 3/4-ton or 1-ton trucks, pour 5 inches.</li>
    <li><strong>Heavy RV, Boat, or Commercial Equipment Pads (6 inches):</strong> Heavy motorhomes and equipment trailers require a full 6-inch pour over a minimum 6-inch compacted aggregate base with rebar spaced at 12 inches on-center.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Ready-Mix Truck vs. Bagged Concrete Breakeven Guide</h2>
  <p>
    Deciding between mixing bags by hand and ordering a ready-mix concrete delivery truck generally comes down to total volume and available labor:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Under 1 Cubic Yard (Under 45–50 Bags):</strong> Mixing bagged concrete using a rented electric drum mixer or wheelbarrow is generally the most economical choice. Ready-mix companies charge "short-load fees" (often $100 to $200 extra) for orders under 4 to 5 yards, making small truck deliveries cost-prohibitive.</li>
    <li><strong>1 to 2 Cubic Yards (45 to 90 Bags):</strong> This is the transitional zone. Mixing 70 to 90 eighty-pound bags requires substantial manual labor and hours of mixing. Consider a volumetric "mix-on-site" mobile concrete service, which dispenses the exact yardage required with zero waste and no short-load penalty.</li>
    <li><strong>Over 2 Cubic Yards (90+ Bags):</strong> Ready-mix truck delivery is strongly recommended. Hand-mixing 100+ bags risks "cold joints" (where one batch hardens before the next is poured, weakening the slab). Ready-mix delivers a uniform, pre-tested mix that can be discharged, screeded, and bull-floated in a single continuous pour.</li>
  </ul>
